("common-eastern-firefly","life","","Males start flashing at dusk, earlier in the evening than many other fireflies, so they are often the first ones people notice on a summer night. Their flashes come more slowly on cool evenings and faster on warm ones. Adults are protected by bitter chemicals called lucibufagins, which make them distasteful to birds and spiders, so their glow may double as a warning."),
("dhole","life","","Unlike wolves, which often seize prey by the throat, dholes usually attack from behind and the sides, pulling large animals down as a team. Packs leave their droppings at shared latrine sites, which work like community notice boards, telling other dholes who lives there. Their many names reflect how widely they once roamed: they are also called Asiatic wild dogs, red dogs and whistling hunters."),
("kea","people","","The bird's name comes from the Māori language and imitates its loud, ringing flight call, 'kee-aah'. Kea are so well known for dismantling cars that conservationists have built 'kea gyms' beside some mountain roads, climbing frames full of ropes and puzzles that give the birds something more interesting to destroy than windscreen wipers."),
("superb-lyrebird","life","","A male's repertoire can include the calls of more than 20 other bird species, such as kookaburras, whipbirds and rosellas, woven together with his own loud songs. Because young males learn from the older birds around them, songs vary from one forest to the next, giving lyrebirds local dialects. During a display, a male matches particular songs to particular dance steps, a kind of choreography that scientists have compared to human dance."),
("thorny-devil","anatomy","","When a predator grabs it, a thorny devil can puff up its body with air, making itself bigger and its spikes harder to swallow. Females are larger than males, reaching about 20 centimetres long, and thorny devils can live for 15 to 20 years in the wild."),
("hellbender","anatomy","","Although hellbenders have lungs, they use them mainly to control buoyancy, not to breathe. A row of sensors along each side of the body, called the lateral line, lets them feel tiny movements in the water, which helps them find crayfish in the dark. Their skin is coated in thick, slippery mucus, the source of the 'snot otter' nickname, which may help protect them from parasites."),
("ocean-sunfish","family","","Young sunfish travel in schools for protection, but adults are usually seen alone. Their main predators are sea lions, orcas and large sharks; in Monterey Bay, California, sea lions have been seen biting off sunfish fins and tossing the fish around. Despite their bulk, sunfish occasionally leap clear of the water, perhaps to shake off parasites."),
("coelacanth","anatomy","","Instead of a fully bony backbone, a coelacanth has a thick, oil-filled tube called a notochord running along its body. Its swim bladder is filled with fat rather than gas, and inside it lies the remnant of a lung, a reminder of ancestors that lived in shallow water. Its brain is tiny, filling only a small fraction of its skull."),
("greenland-shark","home","","Greenland sharks have been recorded more than 2,000 metres down and in water close to freezing, around minus 1 degree. Tagged sharks swim so slowly that one full sweep of the tail can take around seven seconds. Their huge, oily liver makes up a large share of their weight and helps keep them buoyant."),
("vampire-squid","life","","Unlike true squid, it has no ink sac. In parts of its range the water holds only a few percent of the oxygen found at the surface, and the vampire squid has one of the lowest metabolic rates ever measured in a cephalopod, so it needs remarkably little food or oxygen. Its two feeding filaments can stretch to several times the length of its body, trailing through the water like fishing lines."),
("coconut-crab","life","","Like all arthropods, coconut crabs must shed their hard outer skeleton to grow. Before molting, a crab hides away in a burrow, and afterwards it often eats its old exoskeleton, recycling the calcium. They grow slowly and may take decades to reach full size. Although they are mostly scavengers, they can also hunt: in the Chagos Islands, a scientist watched a coconut crab climb to a sleeping red-footed booby at night, break its wing and drag it to the ground."),
("orchid-mantis","anatomy","","Despite its name, the orchid mantis doesn't copy any particular orchid. Studies of how bees and flies see colour suggest it looks like a general, generic flower, which may be why it fools so many kinds of insects. Because females are so much bigger, they can catch large prey such as butterflies and bees, while the tiny males take mostly small flies."),
("hercules-beetle","anatomy","","Not every male grows a giant horn. A larva that eats well becomes a large male with long horns, while a poorly fed one becomes a small male with short horns, so males of the same species can look very different. Despite their weight, adults are capable fliers, and at night they are often drawn to lights."),
